Apple just opened up Siri AI for developers whose app features fall outside its[predefined schemas](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/app-schema-domains) . You can now expose custom actions through App Intents, describe your app’s content with App Entities, and add skills that help Siri handle more complex requests.


This is a big deal. Now that Siri AI is open to ALL apps it might actually become useful. And if it's even somewhat decent (it doesn't have to be the best), it will be a dominant player in the AI assistant space because it's built-in to all iPhones. It's what the vast majority of iPhone users will default to if it becomes even remotely useful.


[Apple announced this expansion on November 3, 2026](https://developer.apple.com/news/?id=gdk2umrf) . It’s a developer preview, so there are limits on testing and availability. But if you’ve already invested in App Intents and built them for your app, then you've already done most of the work.


##
What changed with Siri AI for developers?


Apple’s app schemas give the system a shared understanding of familiar actions and content. A schema defines a particular capability or data type, and a domain groups related schemas, such as messaging or calendar actions.


The new preview lets developers bring custom actions and content to Siri AI beyond those predefined schemas.


That’s the part I’m excited about. Siri becomes more useful when it can work with the apps people actually use. If it can reliably handle everyday tasks across those apps, I think more people will start reaching for it.


That's your opportunity. If you take the time to build a great App Intent setup for your app and Apple finally gets Siri AI right, then your app is in an amazing position. In my opinion, this is a small amount of work for a potentially big payoff. I'll take that bet.


##
How do App Entities, tools, and skills work together?


Siri needs to understand what your app contains and what it can do. These components provide that information:


Component


What it tells Siri


Food-ordering example


App Entity


What a piece of app content represents


A restaurant, food item, or order


App Intent exposed as a tool


What action the app can perform


Add a food item to a cart


App skill


How to approach a more complex task


Find a previous order and use it to help assemble a new cart


###
App Entities describe your app’s content


An[App Entity](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/appentity) represents an app-specific concept in a form the system can understand.


Your food app already knows what a menu item is. Siri needs you to describe that concept and provide a way to find the right item.


Technically,` AppEntity` is a Swift protocol. You can adopt it on an existing data model or create a separate type that represents the relevant information. You don’t have to expose your entire database model.


```text
struct    FoodItemEntity  :    AppEntity    {
static    let    typeDisplayRepresentation  :    TypeDisplayRepresentation   =  "Food Item"
static    let    defaultQuery   =  FoodItemQuery  (  )


var    id  :   String
@Property  (  title  :    "Name"  )    var    name  :   String


var    displayRepresentation  :    DisplayRepresentation    {
DisplayRepresentation  (  title  :    "\(  name  )"  )
}
}
```


