###
Skills provide extra guidance


An app skill is a Markdown file that helps Siri handle tasks involving your tools. It can explain which tool to prefer, how tools fit together, and relevant knowledge about your app.


Each skill has a name and description. Siri considers that metadata before loading the full instructions when relevant.


My advice is to get the tools working well first. If two actions are hard to tell apart, improve their design and descriptions before adding more instructions.


##
When can Siri discover your app’s custom tools?


Custom tools aren’t automatically considered for every request. Apple’s[preview overview](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/siri-ai-developer-preview) describes situations that make them available:


-


Your app is open in the foreground.


-


The person mentions your app by name.


-


Your app has an active Live Activity, such as an ongoing delivery status.


-


The person continues a conversation in the Siri app that already mentions your app.


-


A relevant entity from your app appears in a search result.


These conditions make a tool available for consideration. Siri still needs to decide whether it fits the request.


###
Should you still use Apple’s schemas?


Yes, when an appropriate schema exists. Apple recommends adopting[system-defined schemas](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/app-schema-domains) wherever possible.


Check the schema’s supported experiences, too. Some integrate with Siri, while others are specific to Shortcuts.


You can use schemas for supported functionality and custom tools for the rest. Your entire app doesn’t need to fit one category.


##
How do you prepare your app for Siri AI?


Start with a few actions people would actually want to request. For a food app, that might be finding a menu item, adding it to a cart, and retrieving a previous order.


Build and test those before expanding. A long list of overlapping tools gives you more opportunities to confuse Siri AI and the inference time will increase making the request take longer.


###
1. Review your existing intents


For an eligible custom action, Apple’s[integration guide](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/bringing-custom-actions-and-content-to-siri) shows how` @AppIntent(.tool)` adds conformance to the` ToolIntent` protocol.


There’s an important exception: **don’t add the tool trait to an intent that already uses a schema Siri supports.** That action is already integrated, and adding the trait causes a build error.


An intent using a Shortcuts-only schema can use the combined` @AppIntent(.tool, schema:)` form.


###
2. Make titles and descriptions specific


Siri uses intent and parameter descriptions to understand actions and supply the right inputs.


“Update Cart” leaves a lot open to interpretation. “Add Food Item to Cart” is clearer. A useful description could explain that it adds a selected item and quantity without placing an order.


Describe the parameters just as carefully. Does “quantity” mean individual items or meal bundles? Make that explicit.


Also localize the titles and descriptions for the languages your app supports. Apple’s[App Intent documentation](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/appintent) specifies localized strings for this metadata.


###
3. Make the relevant content discoverable


An action needs the right data. Siri must be able to resolve which menu item or previous order someone means.


Apple’s integration guide covers making entities available through Spotlight and supported queries. It also explains how to associate onscreen content with entities so someone can refer to “this item.”


This is another reason to start with a small workflow. You can check that the content and actions work together before exposing more features.


###
4. Add a skill when the workflow needs it


In[Xcode](https://bitrig.com/blog/what-is-xcode) , choose **File > New > File from Template** , select **Skills Bundle** under Resource, keep the suggested name, and select your app target.


Create a subfolder for each skill inside the bundle’s` Skills` folder, with a` SKILL.md` file inside it. Include the required` name` and` description` metadata, followed by your instructions.


Apple’s[app skills guide](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/orchestrating-complex-actions-with-app-skills) explains the format. Skills provide guidance; they don’t guarantee a fixed sequence of actions.


##
How do you test Siri AI integration?


People won’t all ask for the same thing in the same way.


For your cart action, try requests such as:


-


“Add two veggie burritos to my cart.”


-


“Put a couple of veggie burritos in the cart.”


-


“I want two veggie burritos.”


Also try incomplete requests. If someone says “Add a burrito,” what should happen when several burritos are available?


The[Siri Evaluations framework](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/sirievaluations) lets you script conversations and check which intents Siri calls, which parameter values it supplies, which entities it finds, and how it responds.


Run variations more than once. Siri’s behavior can vary between runs, and one successful request doesn’t tell you much about reliability.


When a scenario fails, inspect the result. Was the action wrong? Was the quantity wrong? Was the item ambiguous? Improve the relevant intent, parameter, entity, or skill, then run it again.


Xcode’s Siri Integration Advisor also provides feedback on your App Intents implementation. For manual app-skill testing, Apple specifies a real supported device rather than Simulator.


##
When can you release a Siri AI integration?


As of **November 11, 2026** , the new custom-action integration is a developer preview.[Xcode 27.3 beta](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/xcode-release-notes/xcode-27_3-release-notes) includes the corresponding SDKs.


The preview supports iOS, iPadOS, macOS, and visionOS on devices that support Apple Intelligence, where Siri AI is available.


You can test through development-signed device builds and internal[TestFlight](https://bitrig.com/blog/testflight-for-beginners) builds. The new functionality isn’t available to external TestFlight testers or people installing your app from the App Store.


Apple says App Store submissions for these experiences will open in **spring 2027** . Its current preview documentation doesn’t provide a date for external TestFlight access. Check the[latest availability guidance](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/siri-ai-developer-preview) before planning your rollout.


##
Frequently asked questions


### Can Siri complete a task across multiple apps?


Yes, when the apps expose the required actions and compatible content. For example, a request could retrieve an order from a food app and share it through a messaging app.


Your custom entities need suitable transferable representations so the receiving app can use the information. Apple covers this in its[custom actions and content guide](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/bringing-custom-actions-and-content-to-siri) .


### Do skills replace the Swift code in an App Intent?


No. Your App Intent’s implementation performs the action. A skill gives Siri guidance about using the available tools.


### Does Siri automatically get access to all my app’s data?


No. Siri can use the content and actions you explicitly expose through the integration. Choose what each entity represents and make the relevant information available.


Apple also provides authentication and visibility controls for entity information, described in its[integration guide](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/bringing-custom-actions-and-content-to-siri) .


